2026-09-09|Total Commas Used from 1 to n

Question
- Given an integer n, return the total number of commas used when writing all integers from [1, n].
- A comma is inserted after every three digits from the right.

Approach
1. Start with the first number that requires a comma: 1000.

2. For every power of 1000:
- 1000 to 999999 → 1 comma.
- 1000000 to 999999999 → 2 commas.
- 1000000000 to 999999999999 → 3 commas.
- And so on.

3. For a range beginning at start = 1000^k, every number in that range has exactly k commas.

4. Count how many numbers from start to n belong to the current range.

5. Add:
   count × numberOfCommas

6. Move start to the next power of 1000 and increase the comma count.

7. Stop when start becomes greater than n.

8. This avoids checking each number individually and takes only logarithmic time.

Solution

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    long long countCommas(long long n) {
        long long ans = 0;
        long long start = 1000;
        long long commas = 1;

        while (start <= n) {
            long long end;

            if (start > n / 1000) {
                end = n;
            } else {
                end = start * 1000 - 1;
                end = min(end, n);
            }

            long long count = end - start + 1;
            ans += count * commas;

            if (start > n / 1000)
                break;

            start *= 1000;
            commas++;
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
```

- Example: n = 1005
- Numbers 1 to 999 use 0 commas.
- Numbers 1000 to 1005 are 6 numbers.
- Each uses 1 comma.
- Answer = 6.

- Example: n = 1,000,000
- Numbers 1000 to 999999 contribute 999000 commas.
- Number 1,000,000 contributes 2 commas.
- Answer = 999002.

- Time Complexity: O(log₁₀ n)
- Space Complexity: O(1)
